INV-zudak was closed `satisfied` in May after migrating build_grammars.py and
gitleaks.py -- the INSTALL entry points. The RUNTIME entry points carry the
identical defect and were never in scope, so the invariant read as held while
eight callsites violated it. Its own statement covers them: every entry point
with an asserted "no direct host_fs writes" claim must route every fs-write
primitive through a safety_zones wrapper, and runtime-cli-no-host-fs is one.

THE WRAPPER WAS MISSING, NOT MERELY UNUSED. safety_zones had cache_mkdir,
tmp_artifact_mkdir and install_artifact_mkdir but no user_out_mkdir -- the one
zone whose path comes from the USER (--out) was the one zone with a write
wrapper and no mkdir wrapper. Four of the five cli.py mkdirs sit on the line
DIRECTLY ABOVE a user_out_write / user_out_open_json_dump call: the write was
wrapped, the mkdir creating its directory could not be.

  cli.py:1033  cmd_sketch            shutil.rmtree -> tmp_artifact_rmtree
  cli.py:1450  _handle_files_mode    Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir
  cli.py:1942  cmd_slice             Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir
  cli.py:4490  cmd_compact           Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir
  cli.py:9451  _emit_handler_slices  Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir
  cli.py:10227 run_survey            Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir
  sketch_embeddings.py:1431/1472     Path.mkdir    -> cache_mkdir

user_out_mkdir is deliberately NOT zone-bounded, unlike cache_rmtree /
tmp_artifact_rmtree: there is no zone root to check, since the user names the
path. The wrapper buys ATTRIBUTION, not restriction -- the barrier stops the
taint walk so the write records as a user_out crossing rather than an
unattributed host_fs flow.

MEASURED, AND THE FLOWS DID NOT VANISH -- THEY BECAME ATTRIBUTED:
  before  violated,     370 raw / 11 distinct unsanitized (10 mkdir + 1 rmtree)
  after   inconclusive, 0 unsanitized, and verify-claims' own details reads
          "2190 flow(s) reach that zone but pass through a sanitizer on every
          route"
inconclusive rather than confirmed is the WI-kajil/INV-bitig honest-verdict gate
working: this repo has bash/csharp/solidity with no taint catalogue, so a
negative cannot be asserted -- the same reason 16 of 18 claims are inconclusive.
The snapshot ratifies exactly one line; nothing moved toward violated.

BEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE (the statement is about runtime behaviour, so a proxy
cannot substitute). The item's filed repro re-run: survey --out
<tmp>/deep/nested/tree/map.json still creates all four levels and writes, rc=0;
sketch still drains a pre-seeded /tmp/hypergumbo_sketch_compare/, rc=0. The
rmtree is now ALSO zone-enforced, strictly stronger than the bare call. That
bare call carried ignore_errors=True, which the wrapper does not accept, so the
fail-open intent is preserved explicitly rather than silently dropped.

ENFORCED AS THE RULE, NOT THE EIGHT INSTANCES. The new test walks the runtime
modules' ASTs for direct fs_write primitive calls, deriving names from the live
catalogue. It reuses the catalogue's OWN curated ambiguous_names to drop
bare-name collisions -- without that filter it flagged 21 false positives
against 8 real ones (str.replace x13, sys.stderr.write x3, yaml.dump), the
INV-tapat/INV-maluk shape appearing in a test rather than in production. Stated
limit: an unwrapped os.remove is NOT caught, because `remove` is ambiguous, so a
null there means "no unambiguous direct write".

